<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yom River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yom River is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, rising in the Phi Pan Nam mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> province and flowing south past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a> and Sukhothai to join the Nan River in Nakhon Sawan province, part of the Chao Phraya basin. Its fertile valley supported the kingdom of Sukhothai, whose capital and sister city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> both lay near its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yom-river.jpg" alt="Yom River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_Samrong_District_from_Bangkok_Airways_flight_PG_214_(2025-11)_-_img.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
